7.3.0 (2026-08-20)

    GUI:
	- BREAKING: The project format goes to 5.1.0, with the rebuilt Conditional Eddy Covariance
	          settings. An older engine refuses such a file rather than ignoring the parts of it
	          it does not know.
	- New feature: A "Channel pairing" table in CEC Settings says which CO2 channel is
	          partitioned against which H2O channel. It defaults to one pairing per CO2 channel,
	          each with the water on the same analyser, which is what a site means when it has
	          not said - and is what the engine derives on its own from an unstated project. A
	          pairing that crosses two analysers is marked, because the octants are built from
	          the water and the carbon together and across two instruments those two arrive at
	          different time lags and through different spectral responses.
	          Which of the pairing's two fluxes are reported moved into the same table, one
	          setting per pairing.
	- New feature: Each pairing can carry further species to partition in the same octants,
	          chosen from the gases the site measures. Carbonyl sulfide is the useful case: its
	          plant uptake is stomatal and effectively irreversible, so the stomatal component is
	          a cleaner photosynthesis proxy than the CO2 one.
	- New feature: A "Singularity band" setting, defaulting to the 0.2 Zahn et al. (2022) use.
	          The engine had it hard-coded at a quarter of that.
	- Fix: Conditional Eddy Covariance requires BOTH a CO2 and an H2O channel, and says so
	          when one is missing. It used to accept either and force the choice to whichever was
	          present - and the engine, which needs both to decide which octant a parcel is in,
	          then refused to run, so the project got a full set of columns containing nothing
	          but the error code with nothing anywhere explaining why.
	- Change: The global three-way "Flux partitioning" combo is retired. It said nothing useful
	          once a site could have a pairing per analyser, and it was what forced the
	          impossible choice above. The checkbox is now a master switch.
	- Fix: The three occupancy limits are percentages and the engine now reads them as such
	          unconditionally. It used to pick between a percentage and a fraction by magnitude,
	          so a box labelled [%] and set to 0.5 came back as fifty per cent.
	- Fix: The hyperbolic threshold's tooltip describes what the engine does. It cited MREA's
	          dimensionless H = 0.25 while the engine compared against the raw product, where no
	          value of H means anything in particular; the engine is dimensionless now and the
	          text says so, including that raising H also thins the octants.
	- Fix: The Conditional Eddy Covariance checkbox has the documentation link every other
	          checkbox in its block has, and its tooltip names the columns the engine actually
	          writes - Reco_cec, not R_cec.
	- Change: The "Detect time lag on raw data" control is retired, and with it the choice it
	          offered. It never reached the engine in the first place: this wrote the setting as
	          pwb_detect_on_raw while the engine declared pwb_detect_prewpl, and the engine matches
	          tag labels by exact equality, so the box was inert for every project ever saved
	          whatever it showed. Renaming it would have switched on, for everybody at once, a pass
	          that had never run.
	          It is gone instead, because there is no longer anything to choose. Its tooltip
	          described a decision between raw and block-averaged data and there was never such a
	          decision: both settings ran on rotated 20 Hz data, and the flag only ever selected
	          whether the gas series had already been through the mixing-ratio conversion. That
	          conversion runs before time-lag compensation, so detecting after it puts cell
	          temperature and water into the gas series at the wrong relative lag - and the gas
	          series is the one being cross-correlated. Detection is pre-WPL now, always.
	          Both spellings are removed from any project this saves, so no dead key is left behind
	          for somebody to edit in the belief that it matters
	- Fix: The PWB smoothing width defaults to 5, as the engine does. It defaulted to 6 here,
	       and because this writes the key unconditionally, every project it saved carried a 6
	       while a project without the key got the engine's 5 - two defaults for one setting,
	       with nothing anywhere to say they disagreed. A project already saved keeps whatever
	       it states; only a new one changes
	- New feature: A check that reads BOTH repositories: every PWB key this writes must appear
	               in the engine's tag table, and the defaults the two share must agree.
	               Nothing at build time connects them, so a name or a default can drift with
	               both sides compiling and running perfectly - which is how both fixes above
	               went unnoticed. It skips when the engine is not checked out beside this tree,
	               like the other cross-repo checks here
	- New feature: Every anemometer and gas analyzer carries an acquisition frequency, in the
	               Metadata File Editor's instrument tables. It shows the station's frequency
	               until the instrument is given one of its own - and goes on following it, so
	               changing the station's rate changes every instrument that has not been set
	               apart from it. Stating a lower rate is what stops the gaps a slower
	               instrument leaves in the raw file being counted as missing data; before
	               this, such a column was dropped and its flux was -9999
	- New feature: Every raw column carries an error value, in the Raw File Description table -
	               what that column writes when the instrument had no reading, in the column's own
	               unit. -9999 by default, which the engine treats as missing whether or not a
	               column declares it, along with NaN, an unparseable word such as NA, and an
	               empty field; the field only has to be changed when the logger writes something
	               else. Recognised in the raw file before any unit conversion, because a fill
	               value is only recognisable while it still looks like itself
	- New feature: Absolute limits are seeded per species rather than from one shared "other"
	               pair. N2O and COS used to get the same window though they differ by three
	               orders of magnitude. CO2, CH4, N2O and H2O keep the ceilings in force today;
	               COS takes 0.01 and CO 5 umol/mol, well above their measured backgrounds -
	               CO's is loose on purpose, since urban air reaches several umol/mol and a
	               limit deletes the sample rather than flagging it;
	               N2, O2 and Ar state their own because a generic ceiling would sit orders below
	               their abundance; anything else takes 5000 of the unit trace gases are quoted
	               in, so 5 umol/mol. All in the stored basis - umol/mol, mmol/mol for water -
	               and pinned by a check that no ceiling may fall below what the atmosphere
	               actually contains, since a limit deletes the sample rather than flagging it
	- New feature: Every instrument carries a sampling mode beside its acquisition frequency:
	               "Instantaneous" or "Averaged over the interval". It decides how the vertical
	               wind is paired with that instrument's samples when its cospectrum is built,
	               and only matters for an instrument slower than the station - so it is greyed
	               out until one is given a rate of its own. Instantaneous is the default
	- New feature: Basic Settings carries a missing-samples allowance per instrument, beside
	               the project-wide one. Left at "Same as above", an instrument uses the
	               project-wide allowance and follows it when it changes; the key is written
	               only for the instruments that state one of their own. The rows are numbered
	               the way the metadata numbers instruments - anemometers first, then analysers
	               - and are rebuilt whenever the metadata is reloaded
	- Bugfix: The spectral assessment file test reads the file's own structure instead of counting
	          rows against the shipped sample. It assumed 14 rows per non-water gas from a fixed
	          row 19, so it was wrong for any project with a second hygrometer - which adds an
	          11-row humidity block - and it compared block headers whole, so every trailing
	          token the engine added (groups=, var=, instr=, exp=) made a good file look broken.
	          A block is now found by the word TFP in its header and sized by whether that header
	          says "numerosity", which is how the engine itself reads it. The warning also no
	          longer claims the engine will ignore the file
	- Bugfix: Refreshed the sample assessment file, which still carried a preamble line the engine
	          stopped writing and a block for the retired fourth gas slot
	- Bugfix: Species molecular weights carry six figures, matching the engine. CO2 and N2O were
	          both 44.01 and so appeared identical; they are 44.0095 and 44.0128. CH4 and H2O
	          moved with them, to 16.0425 and 18.0153
	- Bugfix: Gases preselected from the metadata file are given their species' processing settings.
	          They were created with none, so a project wrote no sr_lim, al_min/al_max, ds_hf/ds_sf,
	          tl_def or time-lag windows for exactly the site's main gases - and the engine reads an
	          absent threshold as "not configured" and declines the test. The pages showed plausible
	          defaults throughout, because a spin box falls back to the species default for an unset
	          field, so nothing on screen said the values were never saved
	- Bugfix: A project already saved without those settings is repaired when it is opened. The
	          legacy migration could not do it: it only ran on files predating the gas records
	- Bugfix: Time lag optimization file browse no longer opens a second, redundant file dialog
	- Bugfix: The instrument tables show every row, and each label sits on its own row. The row
	          labels are a widget of QLabels beside the table rather than the table's own header, and
	          nothing tied the two together: the container's height was a hard-coded number sized for the
	          row count of the day, and with the vertical scroll bar off the rows past it could not be
	          reached - the analyzer table lost Acquisition frequency and Sampling entirely. The labels
	          were also left to size themselves, so one taller than a row, such as the extinction
	          coefficients' subscripts, took its extra from the rest and pushed every label below it off
	          its row, which is how the acquisition frequency came to read against Time response. Each
	          view now states the height it needs from its own row count, every label is pinned to one
	          row, and the block starts where row 0 starts instead of half a row above it. The Raw File
	          Description table had the same two faults and is fixed with them
	- Fix: A project file written with Windows path separators can be opened without destroying its
	       paths. QSettings reads INI values through an unescaper, and drops any escape it does not
	       recognise together with the character after it - and every separator in a native Windows
	       path introduces one. Measured, not supposed:
	       "C:\Users\jonmuell\Documents\_data\CH-LAE COS\lae_biomet.csv" came back as
	       "C:sersonmuellocumentsdataH-LAE COSae_biomet.csv". Saving then wrote that back, so a
	       single open-and-save destroyed the path permanently and the engine was left opening a file
	       that could not exist.
	       Nothing can recover it afterwards - by the time QSettings hands the value over, the
	       characters are gone - so separators are now converted before QSettings sees the file, and
	       the project is marked modified so that saving keeps the repair. A file this interface
	       wrote is already forward-slashed and is passed through untouched; only a hand-edited or
	       third-party file takes the copy, and the user is told it happened
	- Fix: The PWB smoothing width accepts either parity. A centred rolling mean of even width has
	       no midpoint, and the engine used to widen it by a sample rather than follow R, so an
	       even value quietly meant something other than what it said. The engine follows zoo's
	       align="center" convention for both parities now - an odd window is symmetric, an even
	       one puts its extra sample after the centre - which makes the paper's own value reachable:
	       hz/2 + 1 is 6 at 10 Hz.
	       The tooltip says what the choice costs. On the reference implementation's data, widening
	       from 5 to 11 at 20 Hz widened the 95% interval from 0.00/0.05 s to 0.30/0.20 s, against
	       the 0.5 s threshold that decides whether a detection is accepted at all
	- New feature: A "Max carry" setting bounds how far a detected time lag may travel to an
	               averaging period that detected none - all three ways a gas reaches its own
	               lag, interpolation between reliable neighbours, carrying the last one forward
	               and filling backward from the next, because bounding one of them alone
	               achieves nothing. Past it the period takes the lag of another gas on the same
	               analyser instead, and failing that the gas's median.
	               In elapsed hours rather than in averaging periods, because the engine's table
	               has a row only where a period was processed: counting periods would reach
	               straight across a gap in the raw files. Twenty-four hours by default; zero is
	               the published rule, under which one reliable half hour can supply days
	- Change: The gap-filling note says what the engine now does, which is the reverse of what it
	          said. A gas's OWN lag comes first in all three of its forms, and a lag borrowed
	          from a neighbour on the same analyser only after them: two gases down one tube
	          still have measurably different delays, so borrowing trades a stale number for a
	          biased one. It also states the two rules a reader cannot infer from the controls -
	          that a lag is never taken from a different instrument, nor from water, and that a
	          gas whose record names no instrument neither donates nor borrows, since nothing
	          then proves it shares a tube with anything
	- Change: The block-length setting is described as what it is, a floor. The engine derives
	          the block per gas as max(this, 2 x that gas's widest search bound), so a gas
	          searching to 25 s resamples in 50 s blocks whatever is set here - a block shorter
	          than the lag range cannot contain the structure the bootstrap exists to preserve
	- Change: The "Speed options" group is gone. Both options cost accuracy and bought almost
	          nothing, and both tooltips overstated the gain by one to two orders of magnitude.
	          "Use approximate CCF" claimed 2-3x for skipping the CCF normalisation; that saves
	          two passes over the series against one per lag, well under a percent, and because
	          the divisor is constant across lags it changed nothing WITHIN a combination while
	          deciding the winner BETWEEN them by unit scale - the four pair the scalar against
	          vertical wind and against sonic temperature, whose covariances are not in the same
	          units. That is a systematic bias on every period, not the "shift of +/-1 sample in
	          rare edge cases" the tooltip described. "Cap AR model order" claimed 10-15% of
	          total PWB time; AR fitting is about a percent of it, and capping under-fits the
	          pre-whitener, which is the step that sharpens the peak the method exists to find.
	          The engine gained a guard band and hoisted buffers in their place, which cost
	          nothing scientifically and are worth appreciably more
	- Change: The "Detect time lag on raw data" checkbox is gone, replaced by a line saying what
	          actually happens. Its tooltip described a choice between raw and block-averaged
	          data; there was never such a choice. Both settings ran on rotated 20 Hz data and the
	          flag only ever chose whether the gas series had already been through the
	          mixing-ratio conversion. Detection is pre-WPL now, with nothing left to pick
	- Fix: The per-gas search windows show the window that will actually be searched. Both spins
	       displayed 0.0 when a gas had no window stored while the engine searched [-10, +10], so
	       the dialog reported a window nobody was using - and because setting a spin to the value
	       it already displays emits nothing, a user who wanted a minimum of 0 could not say so:
	       the record kept its sentinel and the engine went on searching from -10. The sentinel
	       moved off -1.0 too, which is an ordinary bound a user may want to state
	- Change: The HDI prefilter tooltip says what it does, now that it does something. Nothing in
	          the engine had ever read that setting

